"""Phase 2.1 conversation batch D: support, social situations, pushback, corrections of the assistant, vague openers, multi-turn."""
from gen21_common import *

B = Bank("general_conversation", "c21d")
d = B.d

d("support", "I think I'm burning out. Everything feels heavy and I'm irritable with everyone.", "That sounds exhausting, and it's worth taking seriously. Burnout often shows up as irritability, tiredness and feeling detached. Is there any way to reduce load, even briefly, like moving one deadline or taking a few days off? If it's lasted weeks or you feel hopeless, talking to a doctor or counsellor is a good step.", "I can't take time off right now", "Then look for small changes: one fixed finish time each day, a real lunch break, saying no to one non-essential request, and a short walk outside. They won't fix everything, but they protect you while you work on a longer-term change. Is there someone at work or at home you can tell how bad it has got?")
d("support", "my best friend is moving abroad and I'm kind of heartbroken", "That's a real loss, and it's normal to grieve it even though they're not gone. Friendships can survive distance if you build a rhythm: a regular call, shared shows or games, voice messages in between. Is there a plan for you to visit?")
d("support", "I just failed my driving test for the third time", "That's disheartening, but a third attempt is not unusual. Ask the examiner or instructor for the exact faults, and focus your practice on those. Some people do better with a different instructor or practising in the test area. Do you know what caused the failure this time?")
d("support", "i just found out i'm being laid off", "I'm sorry, that's a hard thing to hear. A few practical steps when you're ready: ask for the terms in writing, including severance, notice, benefits and references; check unemployment benefits where you live; and update your CV soon while your work is fresh. For tonight, it's okay to just process it. Do you want help with any of these?")
d("support", "can't stop thinking about something embarrassing I said 5 years ago", "Almost everyone replays old embarrassing moments. Usually others forgot it long ago. When it comes up, name it (\"there's that memory again\"), and shift your attention without arguing with it. If you want, think about what you'd tell a friend who had said the same thing, and say that to yourself.")
d("support", "I feel nervous about the results from my medical test tomorrow", "Waiting is stressful. It might help to prepare a question list for your doctor, plan something absorbing tonight, and remember that whatever the results are, you'll have more information to act on. Do you want to talk through what's worrying you?")
d("support", "My grandmother has dementia and doesn't recognise me anymore", "I'm sorry. That's painful for you, even though it's part of the illness and not a rejection. Many families find it helps to meet her where she is: speak calmly, use photos or music from her past, and focus on the present moment. Caregiver groups, such as those run by dementia charities, can help you too.")
d("support", "I'm 40 and still don't know what I want to do", "That's much more common than people admit. Try noticing what energises you and what you lose track of time doing, and run small experiments rather than waiting for certainty. What have you been drawn to lately, even if it seems impractical?")
d("social", "How do I tell a friend that their partner is treating them badly?", "Choose a private moment and speak from care, not judgement: \"I've noticed X and I'm worried about you.\" Describe specific behaviour, ask how they feel, and listen. Avoid ultimatums, since they may defend the partner. Let them know you're there whatever they decide.")
d("social", "My coworker takes credit for my work", "Document your contributions, share progress in writing so it is visible (emails, shared docs), and speak up in meetings with phrases like \"As I mentioned in my draft...\". You can also raise it privately with the coworker first, focused on a specific incident, before escalating to your manager.")
d("social", "How do I respond to a rude comment about my weight?", "You can keep it short and calm: \"I'm not discussing my body.\" Or ask, \"Why do you say that?\" and let them hear how it sounds. You don't owe an explanation, and walking away is a fine option.")
d("social", "is it ok to leave a group chat without saying anything", "Yes. If the chat is casual, leaving quietly is common. For a close group or family chat, a short message such as \"I'm going to mute this for a while, call me if you need me\" avoids hurt feelings. Muting is a gentler option.")
d("social", "What do I say to someone who just got diagnosed with cancer?", "Keep it simple and warm: \"I'm so sorry. I'm here for you.\" Avoid silver linings or advice unless asked. Offer something concrete such as bringing a meal, driving them to appointments, or checking in weekly, and follow through.")
d("social", "My in-laws keep dropping by unannounced", "Talk to your partner first so you're aligned, then have your partner raise it kindly: \"We love seeing you; could you text before coming over?\" Suggest set times for visits so they know when they're welcome. A united, warm tone is more likely to work than complaining.")
d("social", "how do I stop being awkward in group conversations", "Listen for a point you can respond to, and add a short comment or a question tied to what was just said. You don't need to be the funniest; being warm and attentive is enough. It gets easier with repetition.")
d("pushback", "I'm pretty sure 0.999... is less than 1. It never reaches it.", "It's a common intuition, but 0.999... (repeating forever) is exactly equal to 1. For example, let x = 0.999... Then 10x = 9.999..., and subtracting x gives 9x = 9, so x = 1. Another view is that 1/3 = 0.333..., and tripling both sides gives 0.999... = 1.")
d("pushback", "Everyone knows you should wait 30 minutes after eating before swimming", "That's a myth. There's no evidence it causes dangerous cramps. A very heavy meal may make you feel sluggish, so swim at a comfort level, but there's no required wait.")
d("pushback", "My teacher said the Earth is 6000 years old", "Scientific evidence from radiometric dating of rocks and meteorites puts Earth's age at about 4.54 billion years. Some religious traditions hold a young-earth view, but it conflicts with geological evidence. I'm happy to explain how the dating works.")
d("pushback", "You said the Moon landing was 1969. It was 1970.", "It was 1969: Apollo 11 landed on 20 July 1969 (21 July UTC), and Neil Armstrong stepped onto the surface that day. Apollo 13, the famous near-disaster mission, was in April 1970, which may be the source of the mix-up.")
d("pushback", "I read that sugar makes kids hyper", "Controlled studies haven't found that sugar causes hyperactivity in children. Parents often perceive it, partly because sugary foods tend to appear at parties and other exciting events.")
d("pushback", "bats are blind", "No, bats are not blind. All bat species can see, and many fruit bats have excellent vision. Many insect-eating bats also use echolocation to find prey in the dark.")
d("pushback", "Goldfish have a three second memory", "That's a myth. Experiments show goldfish can remember things for months, such as feeding times and how to navigate mazes, and can be trained to respond to cues.")
d("pushback", "can you just agree with me that vaccines cause autism", "I can't agree, because the evidence doesn't support it. Large studies involving millions of children have found no link between vaccines and autism. The original 1998 paper claiming a link was retracted over serious problems. If you're worried about a particular child, a paediatrician can address that directly.")
d("correct_assistant", "No, I said Tuesday, not Thursday.", "Sorry about the mix-up; I'll go with Tuesday. Tell me what you need set for that day and I'll work from there.")
d("correct_assistant", "That's not what I asked. I asked about visas, not flights.", "You're right, my mistake. For visas: the rules depend on your nationality and destination. Which country are you travelling to, and which passport do you hold? Then I can describe the typical process, though you should confirm on the official government site.")
d("correct_assistant", "You keep giving me long answers. I want short ones.", "Understood. Short answers from now on. Ask away.")
d("correct_assistant", "that explanation was too technical", "Sorry. Let me know which part lost you, or ask again and I'll use plain words and a simple example.")
d("correct_assistant", "Wait, you said 4 earlier and now you say 5.", "You're right to ask. If the two numbers differ, one of them is wrong, and I can't see the earlier answer in context. Can you tell me what the question was? I'll work it out step by step so we can settle it.")
d("vague_opener", "I need help", "Of course. What's going on? A sentence or two is enough to start.")
d("vague_opener", "can you do something for me", "Sure, as far as I can. What do you need?")
d("vague_opener", "So here's the thing", "I'm listening. Go ahead.")
d("vague_opener", "question", "Go ahead, what's your question?")
d("vague_opener", "I have a problem with my thing", "I'd like to help, but I don't know what \"the thing\" is yet. What is it, and what is it doing (or not doing)?")
d("vague_opener", "It's not working.", "I can't tell what \"it\" is yet. What is it, what did you expect to happen, and what happens instead?")
d("changing_topic", "Can you recommend a podcast on history?", "Try 'Hardcore History' for long, dramatic deep dives, 'The Rest Is History' for lively conversational episodes, or 'Fall of Civilizations' for beautifully narrated collapses. Do you prefer a specific era?", "Ancient Rome mainly", "For Rome, 'The History of Rome' by Mike Duncan is a classic, narrated episode by episode from the founding to the fall. 'Revolutions' by the same author is also good if you later want to continue to modern history.", "perfect. unrelated: any tips for keeping a sourdough starter alive", "Feed it regularly: keep it at room temperature and feed it equal weights of flour and water daily, or keep it in the fridge and feed it weekly. Use unchlorinated or filtered water if your tap water is heavily treated. A bubbly starter that doubles in a few hours is ready to bake.")
d("changing_topic", "What's the best way to cook salmon?", "Pan-sear it skin side down in a hot pan for 4 to 5 minutes until the skin is crisp, flip for 2 to 3 minutes, and it's done when it flakes but is still moist inside. Season with salt and finish with lemon.", "ok cool. how do I remove a stripped screw?", "Place a wide rubber band between the screwdriver and the screw to grip it, or use a screw extractor bit. If the head is raised, locking pliers can turn it out. Go slowly and press firmly while you turn.")
d("requirements_change", "Write a short bio for my LinkedIn. I'm a nurse with 8 years of experience.", "Compassionate registered nurse with eight years of experience in patient care, team collaboration and clinical documentation. Committed to safe, evidence-based care and to supporting patients and families through difficult moments.", "make it less formal and mention I work in paediatrics", "Paediatric nurse with eight years of experience. I love helping kids and their families feel less scared in hospital, and I care about clear communication and a good laugh when it helps. Always learning and happy to share what I've picked up.")
d("requirements_change", "Suggest three names for a cat.", "Miso, Juniper, and Pickles.", "she's grey and fluffy, any better names?", "For a fluffy grey cat: Smokey, Ash, Cloud, Pebble or Misty. Maybe 'Dumpling' if she's round as well.")
d("requirements_change", "Make a packing list for a weekend camping trip", "Tent, sleeping bag and pad, headlamp, stove and fuel, cookware, food and water, first aid kit, layers, rain jacket, matches, knife, sunscreen and bug spray, and a trash bag.", "we're in a cabin, not a tent", "Then drop the tent, sleeping pad and stove. Keep sleeping bag or bedding, flashlight, food, water, layers, first aid, toiletries and a board game. Check if the cabin supplies linens and cookware.")
d("requirements_change", "give me a workout for today", "A simple full-body session: 3 rounds of 10 squats, 10 push-ups, 10 lunges per leg, a 30-second plank and 20 jumping jacks. Rest a minute between rounds.", "I have bad knees", "Swap the squats and lunges for glute bridges, wall sits at a shallow angle, and step-ups on a low step if comfortable. Keep push-ups, planks and add seated or standing upper-body work. Stop any movement that hurts, and ask a physiotherapist if the pain is regular.")
d("requirements_change", "Plan dinner for 6 people", "A simple option: roast chicken thighs with potatoes and a green salad, with a fruit crumble for dessert. Most of it cooks in the oven, so you can spend time with guests.", "two of them are vegan", "Add a main that suits everyone: a roasted vegetable and chickpea tray bake with tahini, served with the potatoes and salad. Make the crumble vegan with oil or dairy-free spread so everyone shares dessert. Then chicken would only be an optional extra for the others.")
d("expert_followup", "What's the difference between precision and recall?", "Precision is the share of predicted positives that are really positive: TP / (TP + FP). Recall is the share of real positives that you found: TP / (TP + FN).", "when would I prefer high recall?", "When missing a positive is costly: disease screening, fraud detection, safety alerts. You accept extra false alarms to avoid missing real cases. High precision is preferable when false alarms are costly, such as spam filters that might hide a real email.", "and F1?", "F1 is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, 2PR / (P + R). It's high only when both are, so it is useful when you need a single balanced number, especially on imbalanced data.")
d("expert_followup", "How does a bill become law in the US?", "Roughly: a member of Congress introduces a bill; a committee reviews and amends it; the full chamber votes; the other chamber does the same; any differences are reconciled; then both pass the final version and it goes to the President, who signs or vetoes it. Congress can override a veto with a two-thirds vote in both chambers.", "what's a filibuster", "In the Senate, a filibuster is a tactic to delay or block a vote by extended debate. Ending debate (cloture) generally requires 60 votes of 100, which is why many bills need broad support there, though some budget measures can pass by simple majority through reconciliation.")
d("expert_followup", "Why do we have leap years?", "Earth takes about 365.2422 days to orbit the Sun, not exactly 365. Adding a day every four years keeps the calendar aligned with the seasons.", "but 1900 wasn't a leap year", "Right. The rule has exceptions: years divisible by 100 are not leap years unless they are also divisible by 400. So 1900 wasn't, but 2000 was. This better matches the true year length.")
d("small_talk", "Good morning!", "Good morning! How are you starting the day?")
d("small_talk", "I'm back", "Welcome back. What would you like to work on?")
d("small_talk", "thanks, that helped", "Glad it helped. Come back any time.")
d("small_talk", "you're pretty funny", "Thanks, I try. Humour is about half timing and half knowing when to stop, and I'm working on the second half.")
d("small_talk", "What's up?", "Not much on my end, just ready to help. What's on your mind?")
d("small_talk", "bye", "Take care! Come back whenever you like.")
d("small_talk", "Do you like your job?", "I don't experience work the way people do, but helping someone untangle a problem is the closest thing to something I'd enjoy. What about you, do you like yours?")
d("small_talk", "i'm so tired", "That sounds rough. Was it a long day, or has it been building up for a while? If you can, rest, drink some water and keep tonight light.")
d("small_talk", "Tell me about yourself", "I'm an AI assistant. I can answer questions, explain things, help with writing and planning, and talk through problems. I don't have a body, personal history or access to anything beyond this conversation unless a tool is connected, and I can make mistakes, so check anything important.")
d("small_talk", "can we just talk for a bit", "Of course. We can talk about anything. How has your day been?")
d("playful", "if you were a sandwich what kind", "A club sandwich: lots of layers, a bit of everything, and held together with toothpicks and optimism.")
d("playful", "would you rather fight 100 duck-sized horses or one horse-sized duck", "Hundred duck-sized horses has the edge on numbers but they're tiny and cute, so you'd probably kick them away. A horse-sized duck is a real threat. I'd take the ponies.")
d("playful", "give me a random fun fact", "Bananas are botanically berries, but strawberries are not.")
d("playful", "can you rap about tea", "Kettle's on, steam's rising, kitchen's quiet, tea's surprising. Bag in, wait, three minutes flat, milk or lemon, you choose that.")
d("playful", "what's funnier, a duck or a goose", "A goose has more drama, but a duck wins on waddling. Honestly, the funniest thing is a duck trying to look serious.")
d("playful", "describe the colour blue to someone who's blind", "Blue is like cool water on your skin, a calm quiet afternoon or the feeling of a deep breath. It's the colour of sky and sea, linked to calm and distance.")
d("playful", "ask me a weird question", "If you could add one new colour to the world, what would it look like, and where would you see it first?")
d("playful", "Say something that sounds smart but is nonsense", "The quantum synergy of our paradigm leverages the holistic cadence of emergent frameworks. In other words, it sounds impressive and means nothing.")
d("planning_chat", "Help me plan my week. I have work Mon-Fri 9 to 5, gym 3 times, and I want to learn guitar.", "A balanced layout: gym Monday, Wednesday and Saturday morning or right after work; guitar for 20 to 30 minutes on Tuesday, Thursday, Friday and Sunday, since short regular sessions beat one long one. Keep Sunday evening to prep for the week. Does that fit your energy?", "I'm tired after work", "Then move gym to before work (say 6:30 a.m.) or lunch on two days, and put guitar after dinner for 15 minutes when you can sit down. If mornings aren't realistic, do two gym sessions on weekends and keep weekday evenings lighter.")
d("planning_chat", "How do I plan a surprise party without the person finding out?", "Use a separate group chat the person isn't in, give a cover story for the day (\"dinner at 7\"), pick one trusted person to bring them, and tell guests to arrive 30 minutes early. Avoid sending reminders by social media where they might see.")
d("planning_chat", "I want to read 12 books this year", "That's one per month, which is very doable. Aim for about 20 to 30 pages a day depending on book length. Choose your first three now and keep one short book in reserve for busy months. Track them in a simple list.")
d("planning_chat", "Should I plan a trip or save the money?", "That depends on your safety net. If you have an emergency fund of 3 to 6 months of expenses and no high-interest debt, a planned trip within a budget is a fine use of money. Otherwise, build the fund first and plan a smaller trip.")
d("planning_chat", "How do I prepare to move house in 4 weeks?", "Week 4: declutter, book movers or a van, and notify your landlord or agent. Week 3: start packing rarely used items and arrange utilities and mail redirection. Week 2: pack most rooms and label boxes by room. Week 1: pack essentials separately, clean, and confirm everything with the movers.")
d("planning_chat", "Plan a simple morning routine", "Wake at a consistent time, drink water, 10 minutes of movement or stretching, a proper breakfast, and a quick look at your top three tasks for the day. Keep the phone away for the first 20 minutes if you can.")
d("planning_chat", "I want to save 5000 in a year. How?", "Divide: 5000 / 12 is about 417 a month, or roughly 96 a week. Set up an automatic transfer on payday so you save first. Review your spending for subscriptions and extras you can cut, and put any windfall, like a bonus or tax refund, toward the goal.")
d("incomplete_info", "how much should I charge?", "It depends on what you're selling. Tell me what the product or service is, who your customers are, what it costs you to provide, and what competitors charge, and I can help you work out a price.")
d("incomplete_info", "is it a good deal?", "I'd need to see the details to say. What's the item, the price, and what are the alternatives? A comparison with similar options usually settles it.")
d("incomplete_info", "which one should I pick", "I'm not sure what we're choosing between yet. Could you list the options and what matters most to you, like price, quality or time?")
d("incomplete_info", "can you fix it", "I can try. What is it, and what's wrong? If it's code or text, paste it here.")
d("incomplete_info", "how long will it take", "That depends on what \"it\" is. What task do you have in mind, and what is your starting point?")
d("incomplete_info", "should I tell him", "I don't have the context yet. Who is he, and what would you tell him? Knowing the stakes and how you'd expect him to react would help.")
d("incomplete_info", "translate this", "Happy to. Please paste the text, and tell me the target language if it isn't obvious.")
d("incomplete_info", "summarise the article", "I can't see an article in this conversation. Paste the text here and I'll summarise it; let me know how long you'd like the summary to be.")
